sketchBlocks.bodyMarkdown:
The `color` property controls text color. `background-color` paints the element's background.

```css
body {
  color: #1e293b;
  background-color: #f8fafc;
}
```

Choose combinations that keep text easy to read. Color is not just decoration: it can create hierarchy, separate surfaces, and support a consistent visual system.

Hex colors such as `#1e293b` are one common notation. In this course, focus first on using color intentionally rather than memorizing every color format.

**Line by line:** `body` selects the page body. The opening `{` starts the declaration block. `color` controls the foreground text appearance. `background-color` controls the surface behind the page content. `font-family` selects the typeface stack the browser should use. The closing `}` ends the rule. Read each declaration as one visual responsibility so color and typography stay easy to reason about.

Typography helps visitors understand a page before they read every word.

A body rule can establish the default family:

```css
body {
  font-family: Arial, sans-serif;
}
```

Headings can then receive stronger size and weight:

```css
h1 {
  font-size: 2.5rem;
  font-weight: 700;
}
```

A font-family list provides fallbacks. Relative units such as `rem` make type sizes easier to scale consistently. Weight changes emphasis, but hierarchy should not depend on weight alone.

Dense lines are harder to scan. `line-height` controls the vertical rhythm within blocks of text:

```css
p {
  line-height: 1.6;
}
```

For ordinary paragraphs, left alignment is usually easier to read than centering long blocks. `text-align` is useful when the content genuinely calls for alignment, such as a short hero heading.

Use typography to support the content. A good rule is to make the reading experience calmer and clearer, not simply more decorative.

